  PHASE 2 IS THE QUESTION. Its signature is aligned and outside growing
  at the SAME exponent -- separation zero is the floor:
  binary     aligned 0.34  outside 0.22  separation +0.13 +/- 0.09 (1.3 SE)  final aligned 0.07122
  graded     aligned 0.54  outside 0.50  separation +0.04 +/- 0.21 (0.2 SE)  final aligned 0.08357
  graded-1   aligned 0.56  outside 0.41  separation +0.16 +/- 0.10 (1.6 SE)  final aligned 0.08921

  PAIRED ON SEED, which is the gate. dF1 is the mean of the last four
  windows rather than one, because one window carries an SE of ~27 Hz:
  arm        final aligned vs binary      tail dF1 vs binary (Hz)
  graded     +0.01235 +/- 0.01745 (+0.7 SE)   -19.1 +/- 35.3 (-0.5 SE)
  graded-1   +0.01799 +/- 0.01467 (+1.2 SE)   -13.9 +/- 33.4 (-0.4 SE)

  THE FLOOR IS THE RULE'S, NOT THE CRITERION'S. No graded arm ends with
  more aligned bias at 2 SE while also not delivering less. Handing node
  perturbation the magnitude it was discarding does not move its variance
  floor -- which is what Hiratani's analysis says should happen, since the
  floor is set by the perturbation noise the estimator injects and not by
  the resolution of the signal it reads.

  The second-half separation exponents above are printed as a DIAGNOSTIC
  and must not be read as a result: that window is a fixed trial index, so
  an arm which merely learns slower scores well on it while ending worse.
